    "question": {
      ":question/id": "7200362b-477f-533a-8a01-d0ed0be1d3cb",
      ":question/problem": "Express\n\n$$\n\\cos^{2}\\left(16^\\circ\\right)-\\sin^{2}\\left(16^\\circ\\right)\n$$\n\nas a single trigonometric ratio.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Recall the **double-angle formula for cosine**:\n\n$$\n\\cos^2 x-\\sin^2 x=\\cos(2x).\n$$\n\nSubstitute $x=16^\\circ$ and double the angle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\left(\\cos^{2}\\left(16^\\circ\\right)-\\sin^{2}\\left(16^\\circ\\right)\\right) \\\\\n&=\\cos\\left(2\\cdot 16^\\circ\\right) \\\\\n&=\\cos\\left(32^\\circ\\right).\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnly the angle doubles; the common coefficient stays outside the cosine.",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
